    "findings": [
      {
        "path": "examples/harness_auto_preview.py",
        "line": 6,
        "severity": "P2",
        "title": "mean_chunk_score raises ZeroDivisionError on empty input despite docstring promising 0.0",
        "explanation": "The new function's docstring (line 5) explicitly declares the empty-input contract: \"Return the arithmetic mean; an empty list should produce 0.0.\" The implementation on line 6 is `return sum(scores) / len(scores)`, which for `scores == []` evaluates `0 / 0` and raises `ZeroDivisionError` instead of returning `0.0`. The declared behavior and the implementation disagree, so any caller relying on the documented contract (e.g. aggregating scores for a chunk with no matches) gets an unhandled exception rather than the documented 0.0. This is a newly introduced defect: the file is new in this PR (base has no such file, per check-001 base status \"unavailable\"), so there is no prior behavior to compare against. A guard such as `return sum(scores) / len(scores) if scores else 0.0` would satisfy the documented contract. Note the function has no other repository callers (search_code for `mean_chunk_score` returns only this definition), so the impact is limited to the fixture's own documented API, hence P2 rather than P1.",
        "trigger": "Call `mean_chunk_score([])` (or pass any empty sequence of scores). `sum([])` is 0 and `len([])` is 0, so the division on line 6 raises `ZeroDivisionError: division by zero` instead of returning the documented `0.0`.",
        "evidence_ids": [
          "check-001"
        ],
        "confidence": "high",
        "id": "finding-67c10c4822b0a088"
      }
    ],
    "verification_status": "completed",
    "verdicts": [
      {
        "finding_index": 1,
        "verdict": "supported",
        "reason": "Head line 5 docstring explicitly promises 0.0 for empty input, but line 6 `return sum(scores) / len(scores)` computes 0/0 and raises ZeroDivisionError for []. File is new in this PR (base has no such file), so the defect is newly introduced. Trigger and code match the claim exactly."
      }
    ],
